From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thecorporatetractor <marco@azarguard.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 17:42:02 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] core: add evaluate bag_unpack

evaluate bag_unpack(Column [, OutputColumnPrefix] [, columnsConflict])
[: (schema)]

One output column per bag property, named OutputColumnPrefix + key, after the
input's other columns; the source column is removed. Without a schema the
unpacked columns are dynamic (as in Kusto) and ordered by first appearance
of their key across rows. With a schema clause the output is exactly the
listed columns: input columns by name, the rest from the bag property
(prefix stripped) converted to the declared type; missing ones are null.
columnsConflict: 'error' (default) fails when a key matches an existing
column, 'replace_source' takes the bag value where the bag has the key,
'keep_source' keeps the input value.

The output schema depends on the data, which Kusto.Language cannot know: it
types the operator as its input minus the bag column and later references
to unpacked columns as unknown. So before translation BabyKustoEngine runs
a discovery query for each bag_unpack without a schema (the pipe's input,
after the statements before it, projected to the bag's keys), rewrites the
query text with an explicit schema clause and parses it again. Everything
after the plugin then binds against real columns.

The evaluate dispatch moves to IRTranslator.Evaluate.cs (a switch over the
plugin name); ipv4_lookup/ipv6_lookup are unchanged.

Limits: the input is evaluated twice (discovery + query); the input must be
self-contained (no parameters of an enclosing user-defined function);
Kusto.Language binds only two positional arguments, so ignoredProperties
cannot be passed (the evaluator supports it if a later Kusto.Language does);
the bag argument must be a column (Kusto's rule), e.g. extend b =
parse_json(s) | evaluate bag_unpack(b).
